    </div>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl mb-10">
      <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Table of contents</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-blue-700">
        <li><a href="#what-is-pr" class="hover:underline">What PR gives you, and what it doesn't</a></li>
        <li><a href="#routes" class="hover:underline">Every route to Canadian PR at a glance</a></li>
        <li><a href="#express-entry" class="hover:underline">Express Entry</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pnp" class="hover:underline">Provincial Nominee Programs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#quebec" class="hover:underline">Quebec's own system</a></li>
        <li><a href="#regional" class="hover:underline">Atlantic, rural and francophone pathways</a></li>
        <li><a href="#family" class="hover:underline">Family sponsorship</a></li>
        <li><a href="#paused" class="hover:underline">Caregivers, entrepreneurs and humanitarian routes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#which-route" class="hover:underline">Which route fits you?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#steps" class="hover:underline">How to apply, step by step</a></li>
        <li><a href="#inside-canada" class="hover:underline">How to get PR from inside Canada</a></li>
        <li><a href="#costs" class="hover:underline">Costs and timelines</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bc" class="hover:underline">How British Columbia fits in</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mistakes" class="hover:underline">Common mistakes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">FAQ</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        If you are asking "how do I become a PR in Canada?", the honest answer is that there is no single application. Canada has more than a dozen permanent residence programs, each with its own eligibility rules, and several were closed or paused in 2025 and 2026. This guide explains how to become a permanent resident in Canada through every route that is open as of October 2026, which profile suits which route, and the steps from your first language test to your PR card. It is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-is-pr" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Canadian Permanent Residence Gives You (and What It Doesn't)</h2>

      <p>
        A permanent resident (PR) is someone who has been given permanent resident status but is not a Canadian citizen. According to IRCC, permanent residents can:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>live, work or study anywhere in Canada, for any employer;</li>
        <li>receive most social benefits citizens receive, including health care coverage (provincial rules vary);</li>
        <li>apply for Canadian citizenship once they meet the requirements;</li>
        <li>be protected under Canadian law and the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Permanent residents <strong>cannot</strong> vote or run for political office, and cannot hold some jobs that need a high-level security clearance. They pay taxes and must respect all laws, like everyone else.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">The residency obligation</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">To keep PR status, you must be physically present in Canada for at least <strong>730 days in any five-year period</strong>. The days do not need to be continuous, and some time abroad can count, for example time spent accompanying a Canadian citizen spouse. An expired PR card does not end your status, but failing the residency obligation can lead to losing it after an official decision. Citizenship is different: it has no residency obligation once granted.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="routes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Every Route to Canadian PR at a Glance</h2>

      <p>The table below shows each main pathway and its status as of October 18, 2026. 2026 targets come from the federal 2026–2028 Immigration Levels Plan.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Route</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Who it is for</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Job offer needed?</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Status, Oct 2026</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Express Entry (FSW, CEC, FST)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Skilled workers inside or outside Canada</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No (FST needs an offer or a Canadian trade certificate)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open; 109,000 Federal High Skilled target</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Provincial Nominee Programs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">People a province selects for its labour market</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Usually yes</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open; 91,500 target</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Quebec selection (PSTQ, PEQ)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">People settling in Quebec</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not always</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open; Quebec sets its own levels</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Atlantic Immigration Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Workers and graduates in NB, NS, PEI, NL</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes, from a designated employer</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open; 4,000 target</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Rural and Francophone Community pilots</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Workers in 18 designated communities</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes, from a designated employer</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Spouse, partner and child sponsorship</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Family of citizens and PRs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open; 69,000 target</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Parents and grandparents</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Parents and grandparents of citizens and PRs</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No new intake; existing files processed</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Caregiver pilots</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Home care workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Closed to new applications</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Start-up Visa, Self-employed</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Founders and self-employed people</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Paused</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Refugee and humanitarian</td><td class="px-4 py-3">People needing protection, or with exceptional circumstances</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Open, case by case</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="express-entry" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Express Entry: The Main Federal Route</h2>

      <p>
        Express Entry is not a program in itself but an online system that manages three federal programs. You submit a profile, IRCC ranks you with the Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS), and it invites the top-ranked candidates in regular rounds. Only invited candidates can apply.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Program</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Work experience</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Language</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Other</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Canadian Experience Class (CEC)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1 year in Canada within the last 3 years, TEER 0–3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7 for TEER 0–1; CLB 5 for TEER 2–3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">No education or proof of funds requirement</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Federal Skilled Worker (FSW)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1 year continuous within the last 10 years, TEER 0–3, in or outside Canada</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Secondary education; 67/100 on the selection grid; proof of funds</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Federal Skilled Trades (FST)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2 years within the last 5 years in an eligible trade</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 5 speaking and listening, CLB 4 reading and writing</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A one-year job offer or a Canadian certificate of qualification</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">CRS scores and category-based draws</h3>
      <p>
        The CRS awards up to 1,200 points for age, education, language, Canadian work experience, a spouse's profile, and additional factors such as a provincial nomination (600 points), strong French (25 or 50 points), Canadian post-secondary study (15 or 30) or a sibling in Canada (15). IRCC runs general rounds, program-specific rounds (CEC, PNP) and category-based rounds for candidates with French or experience in targeted occupations. By our tally of IRCC's rounds data from January 1 to October 16, 2026, the lowest scores invited ranged from 507 to 523 in CEC rounds, 382 to 435 in French-language rounds, 467 to 477 in healthcare and trades rounds, and 697 to 805 in PNP rounds. For the 10 categories used in 2026, see our <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry category guide</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Change coming on November 3, 2026</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">IRCC announced on October 16, 2026 that the CRS will award new points for Canadian work experience and job offers in <strong>high-wage occupations</strong>: occupations whose typical (median) wage is at least 1.3 times the median wage of the overall Canadian workforce. IRCC says this is based on the occupation's typical earnings, not your own salary. Higher tiers apply at 1.5 and 2 times the median. One year of Canadian experience at those levels can earn 20, 40 or 70 extra points on top of existing experience points, and a qualifying job offer can earn 50, 70 or 100 points. You get whichever of the two gives more, not both. A labour market impact assessment (LMIA) will no longer be needed for a qualifying job offer. IRCC says profiles will be recalculated, which could take up to two weeks, and invitation rounds will pause during that time. If you have a job offer, check that your profile details are complete before November 3.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Proof of funds</h3>
      <p>
        FSW and FST candidates must show settlement funds unless they are authorized to work in Canada and have a valid job offer. CEC candidates are exempt. IRCC's current table (marked "Updated July 7, 2025" and still in effect on the page as of October 16, 2026):
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Family members</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Funds needed (CAD)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Family members</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Funds needed (CAD)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">1</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$15,263</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$32,168</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">2</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$19,001</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$36,280</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$23,360</td><td class="px-4 py-3">7</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$40,392</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">4</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$28,362</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Each extra person</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$4,112</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">IRCC usually revises these amounts once a year. Check the table again on the day you submit your profile and your application.</p>

      <h2 id="pnp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Provincial Nominee Programs: Getting Canadian PR Through a Province</h2>

      <p>
        Every province and territory except Quebec and Nunavut runs a Provincial Nominee Program (PNP). The province selects you, issues a nomination, and you then apply to IRCC for PR. PNPs are the largest economic category in the 2026 plan, with 91,500 admissions targeted. Each province receives a yearly nomination allocation; in 2026 Ontario had the largest (14,119), and British Columbia's rose to 6,254 in August. Our <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">province-by-province comparison</a> covers each one.
      </p>
      <p>Provincial streams come in two types:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Enhanced (Express Entry-linked) streams.</strong> You must also qualify for FSW, CEC or FST and have an Express Entry profile. A nomination adds 600 CRS points, which has been enough for an invitation in every 2026 PNP round. You then apply through Express Entry, which IRCC aims to process in about six months.</li>
        <li><strong>Base streams.</strong> These are for people who meet the province's criteria but not a federal Express Entry program, for example because of language scores or occupation level. After nomination you apply through IRCC's Permanent Residence Portal, which usually takes longer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Most provincial worker streams need a job offer or work history in the province, and you must genuinely intend to live there. For the basics, read <a href="/articles/what-is-pnp-provincial-nominee-program" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">What Is the Provincial Nominee Program?</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="quebec" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Quebec's Own Immigration System</h2>

      <p>
        Quebec chooses its own economic immigrants under a federal–provincial agreement, so it has no PNP. You first apply to Quebec for a <strong>Quebec Selection Certificate (CSQ)</strong>, then apply to IRCC for PR, which handles medical, criminal and security checks. Quebec's economic levels are set in Quebec's own plan, not in the federal one.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>PSTQ (Programme de sélection des travailleurs qualifiés).</strong> The main skilled worker program. You submit an expression of interest in Quebec's Arrima portal and wait for an invitation; Quebec has been issuing invitations across its four streams through 2026.</li>
        <li><strong>PEQ (Programme de l'expérience québécoise).</strong> Abolished in November 2025, then reopened temporarily from July 2, 2026 to July 2, 2028. The first intake runs until October 31, 2026 and is limited to people who met the requirements on November 19, 2025 (a qualifying Quebec diploma, or at least two years of skilled work in Quebec).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Quebec's programs rely heavily on French. If Quebec is not your goal, Express Entry's French category and francophone pilots outside Quebec reward French too.</p>

      <h2 id="regional" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Atlantic, Rural and Francophone Pathways</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Atlantic Immigration Program (AIP)</h3>
      <p>
        The AIP is an employer-driven route for skilled workers and recent graduates of Atlantic Canadian institutions who want to live in New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, Prince Edward Island or Newfoundland and Labrador. The steps are: get a job offer from an employer the province has designated, have the province endorse the offer, then apply to IRCC. You can apply from abroad or as a temporary resident in Canada. The 2026 target is 4,000 admissions, so space is limited and provinces set their own priorities for endorsements.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Rural Community Immigration Pilot (RCIP) and Francophone Community Immigration Pilot (FCIP)</h3>
      <p>
        Both pilots work the same way: you get a job offer from an employer designated by a participating community, the community recommends you, and you apply to IRCC. You can get a work permit while you wait. FCIP requires French. IRCC lists 18 communities:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>RCIP:</strong> Pictou County (NS); North Bay, Sudbury, Timmins, Sault Ste. Marie and Thunder Bay (ON); Steinbach, Altona/Rhineland and Brandon (MB); Moose Jaw (SK); Claresholm (AB); West Kootenay, North Okanagan Shuswap and Peace Liard (BC).</li>
        <li><strong>FCIP:</strong> Acadian Peninsula (NB); Sudbury, Timmins and Superior East Region (ON); St. Pierre Jolys (MB); Kelowna (BC).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each community publishes its own priority occupations and intake periods. Check the community's website before you look for work there.</p>

      <h2 id="family" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Family Sponsorship</h2>

      <p>
        A Canadian citizen or permanent resident aged 18 or older can sponsor certain relatives, as long as they meet IRCC's sponsor requirements and sign an undertaking to support the person.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Spouse, common-law or conjugal partner.</strong> Open, with 69,000 admissions targeted for spouses, partners and children in 2026. Partners who already live together in Canada can apply in the "spouse or common-law partner in Canada" class; they must live with the sponsor and generally hold valid temporary status. In-Canada applicants can usually apply for an open work permit while they wait.</li>
        <li><strong>Dependent children.</strong> Children can be sponsored directly or included in a parent's application if they meet IRCC's definition of a dependent child.</li>
        <li><strong>Parents and grandparents.</strong> Since a July 15, 2026 notice, IRCC is not accepting new interest to sponsor forms or inviting sponsors to apply "until further notice", and continues to process existing applications (15,000 admissions targeted in 2026). The super visa allows long visits but is not permanent residence.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For bringing family with you on your own application, and every sponsorship option in detail, see <a href="/articles/how-to-immigrate-to-canada-with-family" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">How to Immigrate to Canada With Your Family</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="paused" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Caregivers, Entrepreneurs and Humanitarian Routes: What Is Open in 2026</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Caregivers.</strong> IRCC lists the Home Care Worker Immigration Pilots as closed. On December 19, 2025 it paused intake until further notice and said the pilots would not reopen in March 2026. The older Home Child Care Provider and Home Support Worker pilots closed on June 17, 2024. Caregivers already working in Canada may still qualify through Express Entry or a PNP if they meet those programs' requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>Start-up Visa.</strong> Paused. IRCC stopped accepting commitment certificates after December 31, 2025, and only people with a valid 2025 certificate could apply, by June 30, 2026. The optional start-up work permit closed to new applicants on December 19, 2025. Federal announcements in late 2025 said a more targeted entrepreneur pilot would follow, but as of October 2026 IRCC has published no rules or opening date.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-employed Persons Program.</strong> Listed as paused by IRCC. Provincial entrepreneur streams, including <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-entrepreneur-immigration" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">B.C.'s Entrepreneur Immigration</a>, remain the main business route, each with its own net worth, investment and intake rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Refugees and humanitarian cases.</strong> People who need protection can claim asylum or be resettled, and protected persons can then apply for PR. Humanitarian and compassionate applications are decided case by case for people with exceptional circumstances. These are not substitutes for economic programs; get advice from a lawyer or regulated consultant.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="which-route" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Which Route Fits You?</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Your situation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Best routes to check first</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Watch out for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
