      <p>
        French can change your options a lot; see our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-planning-guide-french-advantage-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">guide to the French advantage</a>. Graduates in B.C. should read <a href="/articles/international-graduates-bc-pr-options-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">PR options for international graduates</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="steps" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Apply for Canadian Permanent Residency, Step by Step</h2>

      <p>The details differ by program, but most economic applications follow this order:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Check eligibility.</strong> Find your NOC code and TEER category, then compare yourself with the programs above. Use IRCC's "Come to Canada" tool for federal programs and the province's own site for PNPs.</li>
        <li><strong>Take an approved language test.</strong> CELPIP-General, IELTS General Training or PTE Core for English; TEF Canada or TCF Canada for French. Results are valid for two years.</li>
        <li><strong>Get an Educational Credential Assessment (ECA)</strong> if you studied outside Canada. FSW requires one, and you need one to earn CRS points for foreign education.</li>
        <li><strong>Collect documents early:</strong> reference letters showing duties, hours and pay; passports; civil documents; proof of funds if needed; and a job offer or licence if your route requires one.</li>
        <li><strong>Enter the selection system.</strong> Create an Express Entry profile (valid 12 months), register with a province, submit a Quebec Arrima expression of interest, or get a community recommendation or AIP endorsement.</li>
        <li><strong>Get invited or nominated.</strong> In Express Entry, you have 60 days after an invitation to submit a complete application. Provinces set their own deadlines; the BC PNP gives 30 days.</li>
        <li><strong>Submit your PR application and pay fees.</strong> Express Entry applications go through your IRCC account; most other economic and family applications go through the Permanent Residence Portal.</li>
        <li><strong>Give biometrics</strong> (fingerprints and photo) when IRCC asks, unless you are exempt.</li>
        <li><strong>Complete a medical exam</strong> with an IRCC panel physician and provide <strong>police certificates</strong> from the countries IRCC requires, following the instructions you receive.</li>
        <li><strong>Receive your Confirmation of Permanent Residence (COPR)</strong> and land: at a port of entry if you are abroad, or through IRCC's online process if you are already in Canada. Your PR card is mailed afterwards; IRCC listed 33 days for a new PR card in its October 14, 2026 update.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Disclose everything accurately. Misrepresentation can lead to refusal and a five-year ban.</p>

      <h2 id="inside-canada" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Get PR From Inside Canada</h2>

      <p>
        Many people asking how to get PR from Canada are already here on a work or study permit. The main in-Canada routes are:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Canadian Experience Class.</strong> One year of skilled Canadian work within three years, with no education or funds requirement. The November 3, 2026 changes will give extra points for Canadian experience in high-wage jobs.</li>
        <li><strong>Provincial worker streams.</strong> Most provinces now favour people already working for an employer in the province. In B.C., that means Skills Immigration.</li>
        <li><strong>In-Canada spousal sponsorship</strong>, if your partner is a citizen or PR.</li>
        <li><strong>AIP, RCIP and FCIP</strong>, which accept applicants already in Canada.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        IRCC also runs a one-time In-Canada Workers Initiative to speed up permanent residence for up to 33,000 workers in 2026 and 2027. It is not a new program you apply to: it prioritizes eligible applications already submitted through programs such as PNPs, the AIP and the community pilots. Keep your temporary status valid throughout. If your permit is ending after you apply, ask whether you qualify for a bridging open work permit.
      </p>

      <h2 id="costs" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Costs and Timelines</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">IRCC fee (CAD)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Amount</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Economic class adult (principal or spouse), incl. $600 right of PR fee</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$1,590</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dependent child</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$270</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Family sponsorship: sponsored spouse or parent, incl. sponsorship fee and right of PR fee</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$1,260</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Biometrics</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$85 per person; $170 family maximum</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Provincial fees, language tests, ECAs, medicals, police certificates and translations come on top. A single Express Entry applicant pays $1,675 in government fees; a single BC PNP nominee pays about $3,425 including the $1,750 BC PNP fee. Full worked budgets are in our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-application-fees-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Canada PR application fees guide</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On timing, IRCC's Express Entry service standard is to process most complete applications within six months. Base PNP and family applications usually take longer, and preparation plus the wait for an invitation or nomination often adds months. Stage-by-stage estimates are in <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">How Long Does It Take to Get PR in Canada?</a>, and IRCC's online processing-times tool has current figures.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bc" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How British Columbia Fits In</h2>

      <p>
        If you want to get permanent residence in Canada and live in B.C., the BC PNP's Skills Immigration category is the main provincial door. As of October 2026:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>A job offer from an eligible B.C. employer is required.</strong> The two streams are Skilled Worker (TEER 0–3, at least two years of skilled experience) and Health Authority. B.C. has no student or entry-level streams.</li>
        <li><strong>Both streams have an Express Entry BC (EEBC) option.</strong> If you also qualify for FSW, CEC or FST, a nomination adds 600 CRS points. Otherwise you apply as a base nominee. See our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">EEBC guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Selection uses a 200-point registration score</strong> and targeted draws under three priorities: Care (36 health, childcare, education and veterinary occupations), Build (certified workers in 9 trades) and Innovate (High Economic Impact, by wage or score). Care health cutoffs fell from 108 in May to 76 in September and October 2026. Read our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-care-healthcare-occupations-list-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Care occupations list</a> for details.</li>
        <li><strong>The 2026 allocation is 6,254 nominations</strong>, and B.C. expects at least 35% to go to people working outside Metro Vancouver.</li>
        <li><strong>B.C. communities in the federal pilots:</strong> West Kootenay, North Okanagan Shuswap and Peace Liard (RCIP) and Kelowna (FCIP).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To see where you stand, estimate your registration score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> and compare it with recent results on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Applying for PR</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Picking the wrong NOC code.</strong> Your NOC must match your actual duties, not your job title. A wrong code can make you ineligible for a category or stream.</li>
        <li><strong>Letting a language test or Express Entry profile expire.</strong> Tests are valid for two years and profiles for 12 months.</li>
        <li><strong>Counting the wrong experience.</strong> Self-employment, most work while studying full-time, and unpaid work do not count for CEC.</li>
        <li><strong>Relying on paused programs.</strong> Do not pay anyone to "register" you for a parents and grandparents, caregiver or start-up visa intake that is not open.</li>
        <li><strong>Paying for a job offer.</strong> Charging workers for job offers is a red flag; read our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-job-offer-scams-fraud-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">guide to job offer scams</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Losing temporary status while you wait.</strong> Apply for extensions or a bridging permit before your permit expires.</li>
        <li><strong>Leaving out family members.</strong> Undeclared family members generally cannot be sponsored later as your dependants.</li>
      </ul>

